WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.797
All of module three was building data structures, one at a time.

00:00:04.797 --> 00:00:07.594
AB-plus tree for ranges.

00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:10.391
An LSM tree for heavy writes.

00:00:10.391 --> 00:00:13.188
Plain hashing to partition across machines.

00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:15.985
Geo-hashing for maps.

00:00:15.985 --> 00:00:18.782
And vector hashing for similarity.

00:00:18.782 --> 00:00:21.580
Each one was the hero of its own page.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.377
But real systems are never one perfect structure.

00:00:24.377 --> 00:00:27.174
They are a toolbox.

00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:29.971
So here is the capstone question.

00:00:29.971 --> 00:00:32.768
A real problem lands on your desk.

00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:35.565
Which tool do you reach for?

00:00:35.565 --> 00:00:38.362
That judgment, picking the right one for each job, is the whole skill of module three.

00:00:38.362 --> 00:00:41.167
Take one Spotify service and seven jobs.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:43.972
One.

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:46.777
Artists want date-range stats over trillions of listens.

00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:49.582
Ranges mean a B-plus tree.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.387
Keys stay sorted, so you jump to the start date and scan.

00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:55.192
Two.

00:00:55.192 --> 00:00:57.997
Cluster similar users and songs.

00:00:57.997 --> 00:01:00.802
That is similarity in high dimensions, where a tree falls apart, so reach for LSH.

00:01:00.802 --> 00:01:03.607
Similar vectors share a bucket.

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:06.412
Three.

00:01:06.412 --> 00:01:09.217
Find hyper-local fan zones, over a hundred fans.

00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.022
That is geography.

00:01:12.022 --> 00:01:14.826
Map addresses to H three cells, then group by the cell.

00:01:14.826 --> 00:01:17.631
Four.

00:01:17.631 --> 00:01:20.436
A live drop, fifty thousand writes at once.

00:01:20.436 --> 00:01:23.241
AB-plus tree chokes on random writes, so an LSM tree takes the surge as sequential flushes.

00:01:23.241 --> 00:01:26.046
Five.

00:01:26.046 --> 00:01:28.851
A viral song floods the cluster.

00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.656
Hash partition on song i d to spread the load and dodge hot spots.

00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:34.461
Six.

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:37.266
A privacy-safe API for outside apps.

00:01:37.266 --> 00:01:40.071
Differential privacy adds just enough noise that no single user shows through.

00:01:40.071 --> 00:01:42.876
Seven.

00:01:42.876 --> 00:01:45.681
Messy third-party feeds.

00:01:45.681 --> 00:01:48.486
Versioned tables let you roll back to the last known good.

00:01:48.486 --> 00:01:51.229
Look back at the seven.

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:53.973
Not one of them is the best structure overall.

00:01:53.973 --> 00:01:56.716
Each is the best structure for exactly one access pattern.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:59.459
Ranges go to the B-plus tree.

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:02.202
Similarity to LSH.

00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:04.946
Geography to H three.

00:02:04.946 --> 00:02:07.689
Write surges to LSM.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:10.432
Scale to hash partitioning.

00:02:10.432 --> 00:02:13.176
Privacy to differential privacy.

00:02:13.176 --> 00:02:15.919
And recovery to versioned tables.

00:02:15.919 --> 00:02:18.662
That is what all of module three was really teaching.

00:02:18.662 --> 00:02:21.405
Not any single structure, but how to read a workload and reach for the tool that fits it.

00:02:21.405 --> 00:02:24.149
Match the problem to the tool.

00:02:24.149 --> 00:02:26.892
Next, a short quiz to lock it in.
